# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
ESSAYS_2 = [
("节约", "Food Waste on Campus",
 "In our canteen, large amounts of food are thrown away every day. Some students order more than they can eat simply because the dishes look attractive. This waste is difficult to accept when we remember that many people around the world still suffer from hunger. Our school has started a campaign to encourage reasonable portions. Students are asked to take only what they need and to finish everything on the plate. The change is small, but the message is important: respecting food means respecting the labour behind it. If we learn this lesson at school, we will carry it for the rest of our lives."),
("环境", "A Low-carbon Lifestyle",
 "A low-carbon lifestyle does not require great sacrifice. It begins with ordinary decisions. I usually go to school by bike, which saves energy and keeps me fit. At home we use energy-saving lamps and turn off appliances that are not in use. We also try to repair things instead of replacing them immediately. Some people argue that individual actions are too small to affect the climate. I understand the doubt, but I believe that habits spread quickly. When your neighbours notice that your electricity bill is lower, they may copy you. A responsible society is built by individuals, not by slogans."),
("艺术", "The Music I Grew Up With",
 "Music has accompanied me since childhood. My mother used to play soft songs while cooking, and those melodies still make me feel safe. As a teenager I preferred loud bands, and my taste changed again when I discovered classical pieces. I do not think one kind of music is superior to another. What matters is the emotion it creates. When I feel stressed before an examination, a quiet piano piece can calm me down within minutes. Music also connects people: at a concert, thousands of strangers share the same feeling at the same moment. That experience is difficult to describe in words."),
("运动", "Why I Play Basketball",
 "I have played basketball for four years, and it has taught me far more than how to shoot. On the court you must observe your teammates, make quick decisions and accept the result, whether it is victory or defeat. I remember a match in which we were behind by twelve points. Nobody believed we could win, but we kept passing and encouraging one another, and finally we succeeded. Sport is not only about physical strength. It develops cooperation, patience and courage. After an hour of exercise, my mind is clearer and my worries seem smaller. That is why I never regard it as a waste of study time."),
("学习", "How I Learned English",
 "When I started learning English, I depended on translation and memorized long lists of words. I could pass tests but could not hold a conversation. Then my teacher advised me to read simple stories every day. At first it was slow and frustrating, because I looked up almost every word. After several months, however, I noticed that I could guess the meaning from the context. I also began to listen to short recordings while walking to school. Progress was invisible day by day but obvious year by year. The key is not talent; it is steady practice and a willingness to make mistakes."),
("社会", "Traffic and Patience",
 "Rush hour in my city is a good test of character. Cars, bicycles and pedestrians compete for limited space, and tempers often become short. Some drivers ignore traffic lights and create dangerous situations. Others wait patiently and let pedestrians cross first. I once saw an old man fall from his bicycle at a busy crossing. Within seconds, several strangers stopped to help him and guided the traffic around. That moment impressed me more than any speech about civilization. Rules are necessary, but kindness is what makes a city truly livable."),
("社会", "City Life and Country Life",
 "People often argue about whether it is better to live in a city or in the countryside. A city offers more opportunities: better schools, convenient transport and various entertainment. Yet it also brings noise, pollution and pressure. The countryside, by contrast, is quiet and close to nature, but medical services and job choices are limited. In my opinion, there is no perfect answer. The decision depends on a person's stage of life. A young graduate may need the energy of a city, while an elderly couple may prefer a peaceful village. What we should do is improve both, rather than look down on either."),
("劳动", "The Lesson of Manual Work",
 "Last month our school arranged a week of labour practice. We cleaned classrooms, planted vegetables and repaired broken chairs. Some students complained that such work had nothing to do with examinations. I disagreed. While sweeping the floor, I realized how much effort is hidden behind an ordinary clean room. When I tried to repair a chair, I discovered that patience and accuracy matter as much as knowledge. Manual work teaches respect for others' labour. It also reminds us that a society cannot run on ideas alone; somebody must do the practical work, and that work deserves dignity."),
("品德", "Honesty Is a Choice",
 "Honesty is easy to praise and difficult to practise. Last term I found a wallet in the corridor. Nobody was watching, and I hesitated for a moment. Finally I handed it to the teacher, and the owner, a nervous freshman, thanked me repeatedly. The experience made me think about how often we face similar choices. Copying homework, hiding a mistake or exaggerating an achievement may bring temporary advantage, but they damage trust. Trust is difficult to build and easy to destroy. Being honest does not guarantee success, but it allows you to look at yourself without shame."),
("探索", "The Value of Curiosity",
 "Children ask questions constantly, but as they grow older many of them stop. They become afraid of looking foolish or of wasting time. I think this change is a loss. Curiosity is the beginning of all learning. When I was preparing a report about the moon, I began to wonder how tides are formed. One question led to another, and I ended up reading far more than my teacher required. The information I gathered was not in any examination, yet it changed the way I see the world. We should protect curiosity instead of treating it as a distraction."),
("健康", "The Importance of Sleep",
 "Many students treat sleep as something that can be reduced when work piles up. They stay up late, drink coffee and believe they are being productive. In fact, the opposite is often true. Without enough sleep, memory weakens, attention drops and moods become unstable. I once studied until two in the morning before an examination and performed worse than usual. Now I set a fixed bedtime and keep my phone outside the bedroom. My grades have improved and I feel more energetic. Rest is not a reward for finishing work; it is a condition for doing work well."),
("心理", "Handling Pressure",
 "Pressure is a normal part of student life. Deadlines, competitions and expectations from parents can make us feel that we are never good enough. I used to hide my anxiety, believing that admitting it would make me weak. Later I learned that sharing a problem often reduces it. Talking with a friend, taking a walk or writing down my worries helps me see the situation more clearly. We cannot remove pressure entirely, but we can change how we respond to it. A calm mind is not a luxury; it is a basic tool for learning."),
("尊重", "Treating Others with Respect",
 "Respect is shown in small details. It means listening when someone is speaking, thanking the person who serves your meal, and not laughing at a classmate's accent. I once witnessed a new student being teased because of his clothes. He said nothing, but his face turned red. Later I sat beside him and we became friends. He told me that the teasing made him afraid to speak in class. Words that seem funny to one person can wound another deeply. Before making a joke, we should ask ourselves how we would feel in that position."),
("自然", "Protecting Wild Animals",
 "Wild animals are disappearing at an alarming rate. Forests are cut down, rivers are polluted and natural habitats shrink year by year. Some species may vanish before scientists have a chance to study them. This is not only a problem for animals; it is a problem for human beings, because every species plays a role in the balance of nature. Individuals can help by refusing products made from endangered animals and by supporting organizations that protect them. Governments must also enforce laws strictly. The earth is not ours alone, and we have no right to destroy it for short-term profit."),
("科技", "Exploring Space",
 "Space exploration once seemed like a competition between superpowers. Today it involves many countries and even private companies. Supporters argue that research in space produces useful technology, from satellite navigation to medical devices. Critics say the money should be spent on problems on earth, such as poverty and disease. Both views have some truth. In my opinion, exploration is part of human nature; we have always looked at the sky and wondered what lies beyond. The wise approach is to keep curiosity alive while making sure that the benefits are shared fairly."),
("文化", "Chinese Food and Its Stories",
 "Chinese food is far more than a way to fill the stomach. Every dish carries a story. Dumplings, for example, are shaped like ancient money and symbolize good fortune. During festivals, families gather to prepare food together, and the process matters as much as the taste. Regional differences are striking: northern dishes tend to be salty, while southern cooking often uses sweetness. When foreigners taste our food, they are also tasting our history. That is why protecting traditional recipes is important. If a dish disappears, a piece of cultural memory disappears with it."),
]
